Nabuuma Recipe Control — V1.2 Real Data Engine
V1.2 connects the operating interface to a real Supabase/PostgreSQL data layer. This is a production-oriented foundation for the first hospitality pilot.
What changed
Supabase Auth with protected application routes
First-login workspace bootstrap through create_business()
Multi-tenant business_id model
PostgreSQL Row Level Security (RLS) on every tenant-owned table
Role-aware write policies for owner / manager / trainer / staff
Real Products → Recipe Versions → Recipe Ingredients relationships
Database-enforced published recipe immutability
Database transaction for publishing a recipe version and setting the active recipe
Ingredient purchase pricing persisted in PostgreSQL
Recipe cost view using the latest effective ingredient price
Inventory as movement ledger, with stock derived from movements
Real QC/service records consumed by dashboard metrics
Staff-safe QR route that does not query or display cost/supplier pricing
Audit event writes for key UI actions
Billing/subscription table retained as integration-ready architecture
Setup
Create a Supabase project.
In Supabase SQL Editor, run supabase/schema.sql.
Copy .env.example to .env.local.
Fill NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY (or Supabase publishable key).
Run npm install.
Run npm run dev.
First-run behavior
A user can sign up from /login. On the first authenticated visit, the app calls create_business() and creates:
one business workspace
owner membership
pilot subscription record
This keeps business creation atomic and avoids asking the browser to perform a sequence of privileged inserts.
Data rules
Every tenant table contains business_id.
RLS is the security boundary; the browser never receives a service-role key.
Staff can read tenant records and create service/QC/inventory events, but pricing/product-authoring mutations are restricted by role.
Published recipe versions cannot be updated or deleted at database level.
Cost calculations do not silently convert mass ↔ volume. Recipe units should match ingredient base units unless an explicit conversion layer is later introduced.
Inventory stock is derived from movement records rather than stored as an editable balance.
Production hardening still required
Before a paid deployment, add:
Supabase Auth email/redirect configuration and custom SMTP
server-side audit triggers for tamper-resistant critical audit trails
rate limiting / abuse controls
automated database backups and restore drills
automated tests against a disposable Supabase project
monitoring and error tracking
billing provider webhook handlers
invitation flow for adding business members
explicit recipe cost API if the UI needs historical cost snapshots
Acceptance tests
Sign up → first dashboard visit creates exactly one workspace.
Second authenticated request returns the existing membership rather than creating a second business.
User A cannot select Business B rows through the API.
Staff cannot create/edit products or supplier pricing.
Trainer/manager can create recipes.
Draft recipe can be published through publish_recipe().
Published recipe update/delete raises a database exception.
Ingredient price changes affect current recipe cost view without rewriting historical recipe formulas.
Inventory stock is the signed sum of movement records.
QR service view exposes method/glass/ice/garnish/ingredients but not cost or supplier pricing.
Product principle
Know the recipe. Know the cost. Control the standard.